            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <p class="post-lead">A family in Newtown Square called us last year with a problem a lot of homeowners around here run into: three kids, one and a half bathrooms, and a kitchen that backed up every time more than two people tried to cook at once. They didn&rsquo;t want to move &mdash; the schools, the neighborhood, the mortgage rate they&rsquo;d locked in years earlier all made staying the obvious choice. What they needed was more square footage in the right places.</p>
              <p>That&rsquo;s the story behind most of the additions we build in Delaware and Chester County, and it&rsquo;s why this guide exists: to walk through what an addition actually costs here, how the township permit process really works, and what separates a contractor who can frame a wall from one who can carry a structural addition from sketch to final inspection.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Types of Home Additions We Build in This Market</h2>
              <p><strong>Second-story additions.</strong> Adding a full floor on top of an existing footprint is the move for lots that are landlocked by setback lines &mdash; common in older Media and West Chester boroughs where lots run narrow and deep. You keep your yard, you keep your driveway, and you roughly double your usable floor plan without touching the foundation footprint (though you&rsquo;re still loading new weight onto the existing foundation and framing, which is why this category needs a structural engineer&rsquo;s sign-off before a shovel goes in the ground).</p>
              <p><strong>Bump-outs.</strong> A 4-to-12-foot extension off an existing room &mdash; usually a kitchen, primary bedroom, or family room. Bump-outs are the most cost-efficient addition type because they often stay inside existing roof lines or use a simple shed-roof tie-in, and they don&rsquo;t require a full new foundation system the way a two-story addition does.</p>
              <p><strong>Sunrooms.</strong> Three- or four-season rooms, typically glass-heavy with either an insulated slab or pier foundation. Four-season sunrooms need full HVAC extension and proper insulation values to meet PA&rsquo;s energy code; three-season rooms skip conditioned air and cost meaningfully less, but they also don&rsquo;t count toward your home&rsquo;s heated square footage for appraisal purposes &mdash; worth knowing if resale value is part of your calculus.</p>
              <p><strong>In-law suites and ADUs.</strong> Accessory dwelling units &mdash; a self-contained living space with its own entrance, kitchenette, and bathroom &mdash; have become one of our most-requested addition types since 2023, driven by aging parents moving in or homeowners wanting rental income. This is also the addition type most likely to trigger a full zoning review rather than a simple building permit, because many Delaware and Chester County townships still restrict or specially regulate accessory dwelling units. Confirm your township&rsquo;s current ADU ordinance before you fall in love with a floor plan.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Realistic Cost Ranges (2026)</h2>
              <p>Costs vary by township, site access, and finish level, but here&rsquo;s what we&rsquo;re actually seeing on jobs in Media, Newtown Square, West Chester, Glen Mills, and King of Prussia this year:</p>
              <ul class="post-related-links__list">
                <li><strong>Bump-out (100&ndash;200 sq ft):</strong> $45,000&ndash;$85,000</li>
                <li><strong>Sunroom, three-season (200&ndash;300 sq ft):</strong> $35,000&ndash;$65,000</li>
                <li><strong>Sunroom, four-season with HVAC (200&ndash;300 sq ft):</strong> $70,000&ndash;$120,000</li>
                <li><strong>Single-story addition, full foundation (300&ndash;500 sq ft):</strong> $110,000&ndash;$220,000</li>
                <li><strong>Second-story addition (800&ndash;1,200 sq ft):</strong> $200,000&ndash;$400,000+</li>
                <li><strong>In-law suite/ADU (400&ndash;700 sq ft, kitchenette + bath):</strong> $150,000&ndash;$300,000</li>
              </ul>
              <p>These ranges assume mid-range finishes. Kitchens and bathrooms inside an addition move the number fast &mdash; plumbing rough-in, venting, and fixtures in an ADU kitchenette alone can add $25,000&ndash;$40,000 over a comparable bedroom-only addition. Site conditions matter too: a sloped lot, an existing foundation that needs underpinning, or utility relocations can push any of these ranges up by 15&ndash;25%.</p>
              <p>For pricing on the rest of the house &mdash; kitchens, bathrooms, basements, and trim &mdash; see our <a href="/blog/home-remodeling-cost-delaware-county-pa-2026/">Delaware County home remodeling cost guide for 2026</a>.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Township Permits and Zoning in Delaware &amp; Chester County</h2>
              <p>This is where projects stall if you don&rsquo;t plan for it. Delaware County has no single county-wide building department &mdash; every municipality runs its own permitting, and requirements differ meaningfully between, say, Marple Township and Radnor Township even though they&rsquo;re neighbors. Chester County works the same way: West Whiteland, East Goshen, and Willistown each have their own zoning ordinances and setback tables.</p>
              <p>For any structural addition, expect this general sequence:</p>
              <ol class="post-related-links__list">
                <li><strong>Zoning review</strong> &mdash; confirms setbacks, lot coverage limits, and height restrictions. Most townships cap impervious coverage (roof, driveway, patio combined) at a percentage of lot size; an addition that pushes you over the line needs a variance from the zoning hearing board, which adds 6&ndash;10 weeks to your timeline.</li>
                <li><strong>Building permit application</strong> &mdash; plans stamped by a PA-licensed architect or engineer for anything involving new structural loads (second stories, load-bearing wall removal, new foundations). Submitted under the Pennsylvania Uniform Construction Code (UCC), which every township in the state enforces through either its own code office or a third-party agency.</li>
                <li><strong>Plan review and permit issuance</strong> &mdash; typically 3&ndash;6 weeks in Delaware County townships, sometimes longer in Chester County municipalities that use third-party plan review agencies during busy season (spring and early summer).</li>
                <li><strong>Inspections during construction</strong> &mdash; footing/foundation, framing, rough electrical/plumbing/mechanical, and final. Each requires a scheduled inspector visit before you can close in walls or move to the next phase.</li>
              </ol>
              <p>For the official permit and zoning framework, the <a href="https://www.co.delaware.pa.us/departments/planning/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Delaware County Planning Department</a> publishes current municipal zoning contacts, and the <a href="https://www.iccsafe.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">International Code Council</a> maintains the model building codes that Pennsylvania&rsquo;s UCC is based on &mdash; both are worth a look before you finalize a design so you&rsquo;re not redrawing plans after a zoning rejection.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Permit Turnaround by Township: What to Expect</h2>
              <p>Because Delaware and Chester County have no unified permitting authority, the wait between submitting your application and getting a permit in hand varies more than most homeowners expect. Based on the projects we&rsquo;ve pulled permits for across the region:</p>
              <div class="post-cost-table-wrap">
                <table class="post-cost-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr><th>Township/Borough</th><th>Typical Plan Review</th><th>Zoning Variance (if needed)</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Media Borough</td><td>3&ndash;4 weeks</td><td>+8&ndash;10 weeks</td><td>Historic district review adds a step for visible additions</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Newtown Square (Twp.)</td><td>4&ndash;5 weeks</td><td>+6&ndash;8 weeks</td><td>Larger lots, fewer variance requests overall</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>West Chester Borough</td><td>4&ndash;6 weeks</td><td>+8&ndash;10 weeks</td><td>Historic overlay in parts of the borough</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Marple Township</td><td>3&ndash;5 weeks</td><td>+6&ndash;8 weeks</td><td>In-house review, generally faster turnaround</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>King of Prussia (Upper Merion Twp.)</td><td>4&ndash;6 weeks</td><td>+6&ndash;9 weeks</td><td>Higher permit volume can add a week or two in peak season</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Glen Mills (Concord/Thornbury Twp.)</td><td>3&ndash;5 weeks</td><td>+6&ndash;8 weeks</td><td>Rural lots sometimes need a septic/well review layer</td></tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
                <p class="post-cost-table__note">These are typical ranges, not guarantees &mdash; a township&rsquo;s queue length shifts with the season, and any project requiring a variance, a floodplain determination, or a third-party plan review agency (common in busier Chester County municipalities) can run longer.</p>
              </div>
              <p>We build permit lead time into every project schedule up front rather than treating it as a rounding error, because it&rsquo;s the single biggest source of &ldquo;why hasn&rsquo;t construction started yet&rdquo; frustration on additions.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Timeline Expectations</h2>
              <p>Realistically, from first call to move-in:</p>
              <ul class="post-related-links__list">
                <li><strong>Design + permitting:</strong> 6&ndash;12 weeks (longer if a variance is needed)</li>
                <li><strong>Bump-out or sunroom construction:</strong> 4&ndash;8 weeks</li>
                <li><strong>Single-story addition:</strong> 3&ndash;5 months</li>
                <li><strong>Second-story addition:</strong> 4&ndash;7 months (you&rsquo;re often living through it, which changes the pace)</li>
                <li><strong>In-law suite/ADU:</strong> 4&ndash;6 months, more if the zoning review runs long</li>
              </ul>
              <p>Weather adds variability to foundation and framing phases &mdash; a wet spring in Chester County can push a foundation pour back two or three weeks. Build that buffer into your expectations rather than treating the contractor&rsquo;s initial estimate as a hard deadline.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>How Additions Affect Property Value Here</h2>
              <p>In Delaware and Chester County&rsquo;s price bands &mdash; Newtown Square, Malvern, and Glen Mills homes routinely list above $700K &mdash; buyers are comparing square footage and bedroom/bathroom count directly against what&rsquo;s on the market in the same school district. A well-executed addition that brings a three-bedroom, one-and-a-half-bath home up to four bedrooms, two and a half baths typically recovers 60&ndash;75% of its cost at resale in this market, based on the projects we&rsquo;ve tracked over the past several years, and it opens the listing to a buyer pool that would have skipped the house entirely at its original configuration.</p>
              <p>In-law suites carry a wrinkle: they add resale appeal to multigenerational buyers but can be neutral or slightly negative to buyers who don&rsquo;t want the extra space to heat and maintain. Second-story additions tend to hold value best because they add bedrooms without shrinking the yard &mdash; a real selling point on the smaller borough lots common in Media and West Chester.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Financing Your Addition: What Homeowners in This Market Actually Use</h2>
              <p>Cost is only half the planning problem &mdash; the other half is how you pay for it, and the right answer depends on your equity position and how long you plan to stay in the house.</p>
              <ul class="post-related-links__list">
                <li><strong>Home equity line of credit (HELOC).</strong> The most common route we see for additions under $150,000. You draw against your home&rsquo;s equity as costs come due (design deposit, framing draw, finish draw), rather than borrowing the full amount upfront. Rates float with the prime rate, so budget for some movement over a multi-month project.</li>
                <li><strong>Cash-out refinance.</strong> Makes sense if current mortgage rates are close to or below what you&rsquo;re already paying, since you&rsquo;re resetting your whole mortgage rate to access equity. Less attractive when your existing rate is well below market &mdash; which describes a lot of Delaware and Chester County homeowners who locked in low rates before 2023, and is exactly why additions (not moves) are the more financially rational choice for many of them right now.</li>
                <li><strong>Construction/renovation loan (FHA 203(k) or conventional renovation loan).</strong> Structured around draw schedules tied to inspection milestones, which lenders require for large structural work like second-story additions. Slower to close than a HELOC but sometimes the only option if you don&rsquo;t have enough existing equity to cover the project.</li>
                <li><strong>Contractor financing partnerships.</strong> Some homeowners finance through a lender the contractor works with regularly, which can speed up approval since the lender already understands renovation draw schedules. Ask any contractor you&rsquo;re vetting whether they have an existing relationship with a renovation lender, and compare the terms against your own bank or credit union before signing.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>Whatever you choose, get your financing pre-approved or lined up before you finalize a design &mdash; not after. A stamped architectural plan you can&rsquo;t yet afford to build just adds a redesign cycle later. For general guidance on comparing renovation loan structures, the <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Consumer Financial Protection Bureau&rsquo;s home improvement financing guide</a> is a neutral, non-sales-driven starting point.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Structural Challenges Specific to Older Delaware &amp; Chester County Homes</h2>
              <p>A meaningful share of the housing stock in Media, Newtown Square, West Chester, and the surrounding boroughs predates 1960 &mdash; plenty of it predates 1940. That history changes what &ldquo;adding a room&rdquo; actually involves compared to a newer build:</p>
              <ul class="post-related-links__list">
                <li><strong>Fieldstone and rubble foundations.</strong> Common in pre-1930s farmhouses and converted colonial-era homes throughout Chester County. These foundations were never engineered to modern load calculations, so tying a new addition into one almost always means a foundation engineer assesses bearing capacity first &mdash; sometimes requiring underpinning before framing starts.</li>
                <li><strong>Balloon framing.</strong> Homes built before roughly 1950 often used balloon framing, where wall studs run continuously from foundation to roofline rather than being broken at each floor (as in modern platform framing). This affects fire-stopping requirements and how a second-story tie-in has to be detailed &mdash; an experienced local contractor will know to check for this before quoting, not after opening a wall.</li>
                <li><strong>Knob-and-tube or early cloth-wrapped wiring.</strong> Still present in some untouched sections of older Media and Swarthmore homes. Any addition that ties into existing electrical service typically triggers a code requirement to bring at least the connected circuits up to current standards, which is a cost line worth flagging early rather than discovering mid-project.</li>
                <li><strong>Historic district review.</strong> Portions of Media Borough and West Chester Borough carry historic overlay designations that add an architectural review step beyond standard zoning &mdash; exterior materials, roofline, and window proportions on a visible addition may need Historic Architectural Review Board (HARB) sign-off before the township will issue a building permit. Ask early whether your property falls inside a historic district; it changes your timeline math.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>None of this means additions on older homes aren&rsquo;t worth doing &mdash; it means the estimate and the timeline need to account for what&rsquo;s actually behind the walls, not just what&rsquo;s on the surface. This is also where the difference between a contractor who&rsquo;s worked specifically in these older Delaware and Chester County boroughs and one who mostly builds in newer developments shows up fastest.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Addition vs. Moving: The Real Math for This Market</h2>
              <p>Every family that calls us about an addition has usually run the numbers on moving first, so it&rsquo;s worth laying the comparison out plainly. In Delaware and Chester County, a comparable move &mdash; selling the current house and buying a larger one in the same school district &mdash; typically costs:</p>
              <ul class="post-related-links__list">
                <li><strong>Realtor commissions on the sale:</strong> roughly 5&ndash;6% of sale price</li>
                <li><strong>Closing costs on the purchase:</strong> 2&ndash;4% of purchase price</li>
                <li><strong>Transfer taxes:</strong> Pennsylvania&rsquo;s realty transfer tax runs 1% state + up to 1% local (2% total in most Delaware/Chester County municipalities), split between buyer and seller by local custom</li>
                <li><strong>A new mortgage at current rates</strong>, which for anyone who bought or refinanced before the 2022&ndash;2023 rate increases means trading a materially lower rate for a materially higher one on the entire loan balance &mdash; not just the incremental amount needed to move up</li>
              </ul>
              <p>Run that against a $150,000&ndash;$250,000 addition financed through a HELOC, where you&rsquo;re only paying interest on the increment you actually needed, keeping your existing low-rate first mortgage intact, and staying in a school district and neighborhood your family already knows. For most of the families we work with, especially anyone who locked in a sub-4% rate before 2022, the addition math wins outright once transfer taxes, commissions, and the mortgage rate reset are accounted for &mdash; which is the financial story behind why addition volume has stayed strong in this market even as home sale activity has cooled. Zillow&rsquo;s research team has published useful <a href="https://www.zillow.com/research/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">rent-vs-buy and stay-vs-move cost breakdowns</a> if you want to run your own numbers against current local comps.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Energy Code Requirements for New Additions (2026)</h2>
              <p>Every addition that adds conditioned space &mdash; bump-outs, single-story additions, second stories, four-season sunrooms, ADUs &mdash; has to meet the energy provisions of the Pennsylvania Uniform Construction Code, which incorporates the <a href="https://www.iccsafe.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">International Energy Conservation Code (IECC)</a>. In practice, that means new construction inspectors are checking:</p>
              <ul class="post-related-links__list">
                <li><strong>Wall and ceiling insulation R-values</strong> appropriate to Pennsylvania&rsquo;s climate zone (most of Delaware and Chester County falls in Climate Zone 4A)</li>
                <li><strong>Window U-factor and solar heat gain coefficient (SHGC)</strong> ratings on any new glazing &mdash; this is where a lot of sunroom permits get flagged if the glass spec wasn&rsquo;t chosen with code in mind</li>
                <li><strong>Air sealing and duct sealing</strong> verification, sometimes via a blower-door test depending on the township&rsquo;s enforcement approach</li>
                <li><strong>HVAC sizing</strong> for the added square footage &mdash; tying a new bedroom or in-law suite into an undersized existing system is a common mistake that shows up as comfort complaints (and higher bills) long after the contractor&rsquo;s gone</li>
              </ul>
              <p>Meeting these standards isn&rsquo;t just a permit checkbox &mdash; a properly insulated and air-sealed addition costs less to heat and cool for as long as you own the house, and the incremental cost of doing it right during construction is far lower than retrofitting insulation into finished walls later. The <a href="https://www.energy.gov/energysaver/energy-efficient-home-design" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. Department of Energy&rsquo;s home energy guidance</a> is a solid, contractor-neutral reference if you want to understand what you&rsquo;re paying for on the insulation and glazing line items of your quote.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>What to Look for in a Contractor for Structural Additions</h2>
              <p>Additions aren&rsquo;t kitchen remodels &mdash; you&rsquo;re tying new structure into an existing roofline, foundation, and load path, and mistakes here are expensive to unwind. When you&rsquo;re vetting a contractor for a structural addition specifically, ask about:</p>
              <ul class="post-related-links__list">
                <li><strong>PA Home Improvement Contractor (HIC) registration.</strong> Every contractor doing work over $5,000 in Pennsylvania must be HIC-registered with the Attorney General&rsquo;s office. Ask for the number and verify it.</li>
                <li><strong>Structural engineering relationships.</strong> A contractor who can name the engineer they work with (not &ldquo;we&rsquo;ll find one&rdquo;) has done this before.</li>
                <li><strong>Local township experience.</strong> A contractor who&rsquo;s pulled permits in your specific township knows the inspector, the typical review timeline, and the local quirks &mdash; that&rsquo;s worth more than a generic &ldquo;licensed and insured&rdquo; claim.</li>
                <li><strong>A real portfolio of comparable work.</strong> Not just photos &mdash; ask what the addition cost, how long it took, and what went wrong. Every real project has something that didn&rsquo;t go according to plan; a contractor who claims otherwise isn&rsquo;t being straight with you.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>We&rsquo;ve walked several Delaware and Chester County families through this exact process &mdash; including a family room addition in Newtown Square that started as &ldquo;we need more space&rdquo; and ended as a full kitchen and living area reconfiguration once we opened the walls and found what the original 1960s framing was actually doing. You can see how that project came together on our <a href="/projects/family-room-wall-panels-newtown-square/">Newtown Square family room case study</a>, and browse more completed work on our <a href="/projects/">projects page</a>. For a step-by-step walkthrough of vetting and contracting, see our <a href="/blog/guide-to-hiring-remodeling-contractors/">complete guide to hiring remodeling contractors</a>.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
              <div class="post-faq">
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Do I need a permit for a small addition like a sunroom?</h3>
                  <p>Yes. Any addition that adds conditioned or unconditioned floor space &mdash; including a three-season sunroom &mdash; needs a building permit under the PA Uniform Construction Code, plus a zoning review to confirm it doesn&rsquo;t violate setback or lot coverage limits.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>How long does the permit process take in Delaware County townships?</h3>
                  <p>Plan on 3&ndash;6 weeks for straightforward projects once your application and stamped plans are submitted. If your project needs a zoning variance, add another 6&ndash;10 weeks for the hearing board process.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Can I add a second story without touching the existing foundation?</h3>
                  <p>Usually not without an engineer confirming the existing foundation and framing can carry the new load. Most homes built before the 1980s need at least some reinforcement &mdash; sistered joists, added footings, or a foundation assessment &mdash; before a second story goes on top.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>What&rsquo;s the difference between a three-season and four-season sunroom for permitting?</h3>
                  <p>A four-season sunroom needs to meet PA energy code insulation and HVAC standards because it&rsquo;s treated as conditioned living space. A three-season room, without permanent heating/cooling, has lighter requirements but also doesn&rsquo;t count as heated square footage on an appraisal.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Are ADUs legal in Delaware and Chester County townships?</h3>
                  <p>It depends entirely on the specific township&rsquo;s zoning ordinance &mdash; some allow accessory dwelling units by right, some require a special exception or conditional use approval, and a few restrict them significantly. Check with your specific township&rsquo;s zoning office before designing an ADU.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>How much of an addition&rsquo;s cost do I get back at resale?</h3>
                  <p>In this market, well-planned additions that add bedrooms and bathrooms typically recover 60&ndash;75% of cost at resale, though it varies by neighborhood, school district, and how the addition compares to what similar homes nearby already have.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Will construction disrupt our ability to live in the house?</h3>
                  <p>For bump-outs and single-story additions, usually minimally &mdash; the work zone stays largely separate from your living space. Second-story additions and in-law suites tied into existing plumbing lines involve more disruption; we sequence work to keep at least one bathroom and the kitchen functional whenever possible.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>How do I start the process?</h3>
                  <p>Reach out through our <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> for a site visit. We&rsquo;ll walk the property, talk through what you&rsquo;re trying to solve, and give you a realistic scope and cost range before any design work begins. If you&rsquo;re in the Media area specifically, see our <a href="/locations/media/">Media, PA service page</a> for local project examples.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>How should I finance a home addition?</h3>
                  <p>Most homeowners in this market use a HELOC for additions under $150,000, since it lets you draw against equity as construction milestones are hit rather than borrowing the full amount upfront. Larger structural additions sometimes use a construction/renovation loan tied to inspection draws. Get financing pre-approved before finalizing a design, not after.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Is it cheaper to add on or to move to a bigger house?</h3>
                  <p>For most families in Delaware and Chester County &mdash; especially anyone who locked in a mortgage rate before 2022 &mdash; adding on is usually the better financial move once you account for realtor commissions, PA&rsquo;s realty transfer tax, closing costs, and the higher rate you&rsquo;d take on with a new mortgage. An addition financed through home equity typically costs less overall while keeping your existing low-rate first mortgage intact.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Do older homes in Media or West Chester need special engineering for an addition?</h3>
                  <p>Often, yes. Homes built before roughly 1950 in this area commonly have fieldstone or rubble foundations and balloon framing that weren&rsquo;t engineered to modern load standards. Tying a new addition into either typically requires a structural engineer&rsquo;s assessment before framing starts, and portions of Media and West Chester boroughs carry historic district review requirements on top of standard zoning.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section post-related-links reveal" aria-label="Related ProBrothers services and service areas">
